ic breast cancer; an intergroup Trial (E1193)</article-title><source>J Clin Oncol</source><volume>21</volume><fpage>588</fpage><lpage>92</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">12586793</pub-id></mixed-citation></ref><ref id="b45-btt-1-3"><mixed-citation publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Spicer</surname><given-names>J</given-names></name></person-group><year>2004</year><article-title>Technology evaluation: pertuzumab</article-title><source>Curr Opin Mol Ther</source><volume>6</volume><fpage>337</fpage><lpage>43</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">15264437</pub-id></mixed-citation></ref><ref id="b46-btt-1-3"><mixed-citation publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Traina</surname><given-names>A</given-names></name><name><surname>Dickler</surname><given-names>MN</given-names></name><name><surname>Caravelli</surname><given-names>JF</given-names></name><etal/></person-group><year>2005</year><article-title>A feasibility study of an aromatase inhibitor (AI), letrozole (L) and the antibody to vascular endothelial growth factor (VEGF), bevacizumab (B), in patients (pts) with hormone receptor-positive metastatic breast cancer (MBC)</article-title><source>Proc Am Soc Clin Oncol</source><volume>23</volume><fpage>796</fpage></mixed-citation></ref><ref id="b47-btt-1-3"><mixed-citation publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Viloria-Petit</surname><given-names>A</given-names></name><name><surname>Crombet</surname><given-names>T</given-names></name><name><surname>Jothy</surname><given-names>S</given-names></name><etal/></person-group><year>2001</year><article-title>Acquired resistance to the antitumor effect of epidermal growth factor receptor-blocking antibodies in vivo. A role for altered tumor angiogenesis</article-title><source>Cancer Res</source><volume>61</volume><fpage>5090</fpage><lpage>101</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">11431346</pub-id></mixed-citation></ref><ref id="b48-btt-1-3"><mixed-citation publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Wagner</surname><given-names>LI</given-names></name><name><surname>Wang</surname><given-names>M</given-names></name><name><surname>Miller</surname><given-names>K</given-names></name><etal/></person-group><year>2006</year><article-title>Health-related quality of life among patients with metastatic breast cancer receiving paclitaxel versus paclitaxel plus bevacizumab: results from the Eastern Cooperative Oncology Group (ECOG) study E2100</article-title><source>Breast Cancer Res Treat</source><volume>24</volume><fpage>5078</fpage></mixed-citation></ref><ref id="b49-btt-1-3"><mixed-citation publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Wedam</surname><given-names>SB</given-names></name><name><surname>Low</surname><given-names>JA</given-names></name><name><surname>Yang</surname><given-names>SX</given-names></name><etal/></person-group><year>2006</year><article-title>Antiangiogenic and antitumor effects of bevacizumab in patients with inflammatory and locally advanced breast cancer</article-title><source>J Clin Oncol</source><volume>24</volume><fpage>769</fpage><lpage>77</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">16391297</pub-id></mixed-citation></ref></ref-list></back><floats-group><fig id="f1-btt-1-3" position="float"><label>Figure 1</label><caption><p>A simplified scheme of the VEGF/VEGFR pathway. The development of blood vessels is regulated by production of several growth factors, such as TGF-&#x003b1; and VEGF, that are secreted by cancer cells to stimulate normal endothelial cell growth through paracrine mechanisms. VEGF binds to two different receptors: VGFR-1 (flt-1) and VEGFR-2 (flk/KDR). VEFG is a potent and specific mitogen for endothelial cells, activates angiogenesis and enhances vascular permeability.</p><p><bold>Abbreviations:</bold> VEGF, vascular endothelial growth factor; TGF-&#x003b1;, transforming growth factor-&#x003b1;; VEGFR-1, vascular endothelial growth factor receptor-1; VEGFR-2, vascular endothelial growth factor receptor-2.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="btt-1-3f1"/></fig><table-wrap id="t1-btt-1-3" position="float"><label>Table 1</label><caption><p>Phase III combination studies of bevacizumab</p></caption><table frame="hsides" rules="groups"><thead><tr><th align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Patient population</th><th align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Metastatic breast cancer<xref ref-type="table-fn" rid="tfn1-btt-1-3">a</xref></th><th align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Metastatic breast cancer<xref ref-type="table-fn" rid="tfn2-btt-1-3">b</xref></th></tr><tr><th colspan="3" align="left" rowspan="1"><hr/></th></tr><tr><th align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">N. patients</th><th align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">680</th><th align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">462</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1"/><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Arm 1</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Arm 1</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1"/><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Paclitaxel: 90 mg/m<sup>2</sup> on days 1, 8, 15</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Capecitabine: 2500 mg/m<sup>2</sup>/day for14 out of 21 days</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1"/><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Arm 2</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Arm 2</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Schedule</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Paclitaxel: 90 mg/m<sup>2</sup> on days 1, 8, 15</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Capecitabine: 2500 mg/m<sup>2</sup>/day for14 out of 21 days</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1"/><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Bevacizumab:10 mg/kg on days 1, 15</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Bevacizumab:15 mg/kg q 3 weeks</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Efficacy endpoints</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Response rate: 14.2% (arm 1) vs 28.2% (arm 2); p &#x0003c; 0.0001</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Response rate: 9.1% (arm 1) vs 19.8% (arm 2); p = 0.001</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1"/><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Progression-free survival: 6.11 (arm 1) vs 10.97 (arm 2) months</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Progression-free survival: 4.86 (arm 1) vs 4.17 (arm 2) months</td></tr></tbody></table><table-wrap-foot><fn id="tfn1-btt-1-3"><label>a</label><p><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="b28-btt-1-3">Miller et al (2005b)</xref>;</p></fn><fn id="tfn2-btt-1-3"><label>b</label><p><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="b27-btt-1-3">Miller et al (2005a)</xref>.</p></fn></table-wrap-foot></table-wrap></floats-group></article>